. Complex SQL — Customer purchase pattern
Orders:

customer_id | order_date | amount
101         | 2026-01-10 | 500
101         | 2026-01-20 | 700
101         | 2026-02-10 | 800
102         | 2026-01-10 | 100
102         | 2026-02-10 | 200
103         | 2026-01-10 | 1000
103         | 2026-03-10 | 2000

Question
Find customers who:
1. Placed at least 2 orders
2. Had their second order within 30 days of their first order
3. Their total purchase amount is greater than 1,000
Answer

WITH x AS (
    SELECT
        customer_id,
        order_date,
        amount,
        ROW_NUMBER() OVER (
            PARTITION BY customer_id
            ORDER BY order_date
        ) AS rn
    FROM orders
),
first_second AS (
    SELECT
        customer_id,
        MAX(CASE WHEN rn = 1 THEN order_date END) AS first_order,
        MAX(CASE WHEN rn = 2 THEN order_date END) AS second_order
    FROM x
    GROUP BY customer_id
),
totals AS (
    SELECT
        customer_id,
        SUM(amount) AS total_amount
    FROM orders
    GROUP BY customer_id
)
SELECT
    f.customer_id,
    f.first_order,
    f.second_order,
    t.total_amount
FROM first_second f
JOIN totals t
    ON f.customer_id = t.customer_id
WHERE f.second_order <= f.first_order + INTERVAL '30 days'
  AND t.total_amount > 1000;

2. Find gaps in employee salary history
Table:

employee_id | effective_date | salary
101         | 2024-01-01     | 50000
101         | 2025-01-01     | 60000
101         | 2026-01-01     | 70000
102         | 2024-01-01     | 50000
102         | 2026-01-01     | 65000

Question
Find employees whose salary history has a gap of more than 1 year.
Answer

WITH x AS (
    SELECT
        employee_id,
        effective_date,
        LAG(effective_date) OVER (
            PARTITION BY employee_id
            ORDER BY effective_date
        ) AS previous_date
    FROM salary_history
)
SELECT *
FROM x
WHERE previous_date IS NOT NULL
  AND effective_date > previous_date + INTERVAL '1 year';

Expected: employee 102.

2. Sessionization problem
You have user events:

user_id | event_time          | event
1       | 2026-09-01 10:00:00 | login
1       | 2026-09-01 10:10:00 | click
1       | 2026-09-01 10:20:00 | click
1       | 2026-09-01 11:00:00 | logout
1       | 2026-09-01 14:00:00 | login
2       | 2026-09-01 09:00:00 | login
2       | 2026-09-01 09:20:00 | click

A new session starts if the gap between consecutive events is more than 30 minutes.
Question
Create a session_id for every event.
Answer

from pyspark.sql.window import Window
from pyspark.sql.functions import (
    lag, col, when, sum
)

w = Window.partitionBy("user_id").orderBy("event_time")

df1 = df.withColumn(
    "previous_event",
    lag("event_time").over(w)
)

df2 = df1.withColumn(
    "new_session",
    when(
        col("previous_event").isNull() |
        (
            (col("event_time").cast("long") -
             col("previous_event").cast("long")) > 1800
        ),
        1
    ).otherwise(0)
)

df3 = df2.withColumn(
    "session_id",
    sum("new_session").over(w)
)

